          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>A <fr:link href="/sss-0006/" title="Monoidal Category" uri="https://stevenschaefer.net/sss-0006/" display-uri="sss-0006" type="local">monoidal category</fr:link> is <html:em>left closed</html:em> if for each <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[Y \in  C]]></fr:tex>, the functor <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[- \otimes  Y : C \to  C]]></fr:tex> has a right adjoint <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[{Y} \multimap _L {-}  : C \to  C ]]></fr:tex> forming the left internal-hom out of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[Y]]></fr:tex>.</html:p>
            <html:p>
That is, for all <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[X,Y,Z]]></fr:tex>, there is a natural isomorphism <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[C[X \otimes  Y , Z] \cong  C[ X , [Y , Z]]]]></fr:tex></html:p>
            <html:p>
There is an obvious right-handed variant <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[{Y} \multimap _R {-}]]></fr:tex> that is right adjoint to <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[Y \otimes  -]]></fr:tex>. <html:em>Note</html:em>: this is likely better denoted with a flipped symbol, but I don't yet know if I can include it in Forester.
</html:p>
            <html:p>
If <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[C]]></fr:tex> is both right and left closed, the monoidal category is simply called <html:em>closed</html:em> (or perhaps <html:em>biclosed</html:em>).
</html:p>
